# The one position a lent C pointer may stand in, asked at EVERY position
# in the file (design.md §4.20; spec § FFI; defect 022, found by the
# ffi-pragmatist at panel 121).
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE. The question is about a POSITION, not about a
# type in hand: `check/builtins.hero` types `s.cstr()` and answers `cstr`,
# which is right and says nothing about where that answer may go. That
# file is also at its decided ceiling with no room. The shape here is
# `check/ffi_sweep.hero`'s, and for that module's own reason: a pass over
# the whole file rather than a question at one call site.
#
# WHAT IT REFUSES, and the count that first justified it was wrong three
# ways. `spec § 13 FFI` says `.cstr()` lends a `str` to C to read **for that
# call**, and panel 122 turned that into three clauses: a lend stands only
# as an argument of a call (R2), no function outside an `extern` group
# answers `cstr` (R3), and no record outside a group holds one (R4).
# Measured after the sitting: **0 of 638** files in this tree change
# verdict, so the whole rule costs this corpus nothing.
#
# The first version of this doc claimed 67 of 67 `.cstr()` sites were
# already arguments of an extern call. It was 66; two of the four
# re-reads were not externs at all — `bind.system(...)` is the NEGATIVE
# fixture `tests/golden/surface-fixtures/externroute/wrong.hero` and
# `as_text.say(...)` is a Heroes wrapper — and the blast radius that came
# with it undercounted sites threefold. A grep line was read where a file
# had to be opened, three times running.
#
# IT ASKS THE VALUE AND NOT THE WORLD (CLAUDE.md §11). The legal position
# is built by reading the call nodes in hand, so a position the AST gains
# tomorrow is refused by default rather than admitted in silence. That is
# what closes `xs.push(s.cstr())` and `Rec(field: s.cstr())`, which the
# first draft of this rule would have allowed: both are argument
# positions, and only an `extern` call is one that reads the bytes and
# returns.
#
# WHY THE EXTERN DISCRIMINATOR IS NOT HERE. It was, and panel 122 removed
# it on three grounds: two qualified calls that look identical differ in
# legality by what another file declares (the ergonomist's veto), no
# language uses which KIND of function is called as a lifetime criterion
# (the historian found none), and it refused three SOUND sites in the
# wrapper §4.19 prescribes as the route out of a module.
#
# THE LEASE IS `check/leasing.hero`'S, since M-held-bytes (panel 124, ratified
# 2026-09-09; panel 125). It is §4.19's FOURTH case, the mirror of the lend,
# and it borrows this file's two instruments — `is_builtin_named` and
# `argument_of` — because its rule is the lend's position rule applied to a
# cell. It was written into this file first, which put it at 366 lines in
# `suite_layout.hero`'s unit against §11's 300, and the suite said so; the
# seam it went out along is the one this paragraph names.
#
# WHY THE LITERAL CASE GOES TOO. `return "lit".cstr()` is sound today,
# because `HERO_STR_STATIC` gives a literal static storage and an
# immortal refcount, while `return ("a" + n.to_str()).cstr()` is a
# use-after-free that builds with zero diagnostics and prints an empty
# line at exit 0. The two are one brace apart and what separates them is
# invisible at the call site, so the rule refuses both rather than
# teaching a reader to tell them apart (author decision 2026-09-09,
# choosing the strict rule over the escape-only one).

use ast
use source
use parse
use resolve
use check/lower
use resolved
use inventory
use check/state
use check/table
use check/lend_extent
use lend_errors

# Whether this expression is a lend: `s.cstr()` or `cstr(s)`. The
# RESOLVER answers, not the text — a name is only a builtin because the
# resolver said so, and `check/builtins.hero`'s own dispatch reads the
# same table.
function is_lend(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, at: i64) -> bool
    return is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "cstr") || is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr")

function is_builtin_named(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, at: i64, name: str) -> bool
    named = match a.exprs[at].kind
        .method                                                                     => at
        .call cl                                                                    => cl.callee
        .name | .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit  => 0 - 1
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index        => 0 - 1
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => 0 - 1

    if named < 0
        return false

    match r.uses[named]
        .builtin b                            => return inventory.table()[b.at].name == name
        .top | .local | .module | .unresolved => return false

# The call this expression is an argument of, for every expression in the
# file. A UFCS receiver IS the first argument (§4.11), which is what makes
# `"p".cstr().hero_file_read(@status)` legal.
#
# THE MATCH IS IN A FUNCTION OF ITS OWN so that the arms which record
# nothing can be a `return`. Written inline in the `while` below, they
# were a `continue` — which the spec forbids for exactly this reason,
# § Control flow: *"An arm that does nothing is a block holding `_ = 0` —
# `continue` is not one"*. In a `while` it jumps past the increment, and
# the sweep hung on its first run.
function record_arguments(a: ast.Ast, at: i64, @into: {i64: i64})
    match a.exprs[at].kind
        .call cl =>
            for arg in cl.args
                into[arg.value] @ at
        .method m =>
            into[m.receiver] @ at

            for arg in m.args
                into[arg.value] @ at
        .name | .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit  => return
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index        => return
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => return

function argument_of(a: ast.Ast) -> {i64: i64}
    into: {i64: i64} @ {}
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < a.exprs.len()
        record_arguments(a, at, @into)
        at @ at + 1

    return into

# A function outside an `extern` group may not answer `cstr` (panel 122 R3).
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT A FLOW ANALYSIS. `return pass(c: s.cstr())` puts the lend in
# an argument position, which the rule above blesses, and returns a CALL
# RESULT, which no rule about lends can see. Both compiling seats at panel 122
# built it: silent at `check`, heap-use-after-free under the sanitizer. One
# signature rule closes the whole two-hop route, and it is measured FREE —
# every `-> cstr` in `selfhost/`, `examples/` and `tests/` is already inside an
# `extern` group.
#
# It has no `written_types` hole, and that was measured rather than assumed: a
# generic body cannot answer `cstr`, because `function escape<T>(...) -> T`
# returning a lend is `error[type_mismatch]` at the definition, before
# `ir/mono.hero` instantiates anything.
function no_cstr_out_of_heroes(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .function_decl fd =>
                if fd.value.is_extern
                    continue
                declared = c.out.written_types[fd.value.result]

                if declared.is_err()
                    continue

                if table.get(c.out.types, declared.must()) == .cstr
                    state.push_diagnostic(
                        @c,
                        lend_errors.cstr_out_of_heroes(source.span_text(s, decl.name), a.types[fd.value.result].span)
                    )
            .record_decl | .constant_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

# A `cstr` field on a record outside an `extern` group (panel 122 R4).
#
# `selfhost/emit/gate.hero` already refuses `cstr` as the element of an array, a
# map or a `T?`. A record FIELD was the hole, and it was the hole the emitter's
# own note sent people through: *"hold the pointer in a `record` and make an
# array of that instead"*. Measured at panel 122: silent at `check`, and the
# binary writes — heap-use-after-free on read. That note is rewritten in the
# same commit as this clause, because a diagnostic that prescribes a compile
# error is worse than one that says nothing.
#
# A GROUP'S record is untouched: there the fields are the header's, C owns the
# bytes, and § FFI says in as many words that a group field may be a `cstr`.
# Enumerated over the tree at the sitting: no `cstr` field outside a group
# exists, so the clause costs this corpus nothing.
function no_cstr_in_a_record(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .record_decl rec =>
                if !rec.header.is_err()
                    continue

                for field in rec.fields
                    declared = c.out.written_types[field.ty]

                    if declared.is_err()
                        continue

                    if table.get(c.out.types, declared.must()) == .cstr
                        state.push_diagnostic(
                            @c,
                            lend_errors.cstr_in_a_record(holder: source.span_text(s, decl.name), field: source.span_text(s, field.name), span: a.types[field.ty].span)
                        )
            .function_decl | .constant_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

# The sweep. A lend may stand only as an argument of a call (panel 122 R2).
#
# THE EXTERN DISCRIMINATOR WAS DROPPED AT THAT SITTING, on three grounds: the
# ergonomist vetoed it because two identical-looking qualified calls differ in
# legality by what another file declares; the historian could source no
# language that uses which KIND of function is called as a lifetime criterion;
# and the engineer measured that it refuses three SOUND sites in the wrapper
# §4.19 prescribes as the route out of a module.
function lent_only_into_c(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    no_cstr_out_of_heroes(@c, a, s, decls)
    no_cstr_in_a_record(@c, a, s, decls)

    # The early exit panel 122 R8 asks for: 637 of 638 files in this tree hold
    # no lend at all, and building a parent map over every expression of every
    # one of them cost 1.3% of `heroes check` for nothing.
    if !holds_a_lend(a, r)
        return
    into = argument_of(a)
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < a.exprs.len()
        if into[at].is_err()
            if is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "cstr")
                state.push_diagnostic(@c, lend_errors.cstr_escapes(a.exprs[at].span))

            if is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr")
                state.push_diagnostic(@c, lend_errors.field_lend_escapes(a.exprs[at].span))

        # **AND A LENT FIELD MUST NAME A PLACE** (panel 164). The lend hands C
        # the field where it lies so C may write back into it, and that is only
        # true when the bytes belong to a binding the program can still see:
        # `make().nsap.ptr()` lends a returned temporary, and C's write lands in
        # storage the next statement may reuse. Measured before this clause
        # existed: it reached the emitter, found no place to render, and aborted
        # at run time saying `this is a compiler bug`.
        #
        # It asks the RECEIVER's own shape and nothing about the world around it
        # (`.claude/rules/module-shape.md`): a chain of fields rooted at a name.
        if is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr")
            if !lends_a_place(a, at)
                state.push_diagnostic(@c, lend_errors.field_lend_needs_a_place(a.exprs[at].span))

            # **AND A LENT FIELD REACHES C DIRECTLY** (panel 166, 2026-09-19). The
            # two questions about a lend are answered by the HEADER and nowhere
            # else: whether C writes through it — the header's `const`, which
            # clang checks against the cast the root decides — and how far it
            # reads, the extent route C declares on the group's parameter. A
            # Heroes function taking `ptr` carries neither: its parameter is a
            # plain `void *`, so a lend from an immutable root failed inside
            # clang at the wrapper's call, exit 2, and a lend from a `@` root
            # reached C with nothing checked about it. Measured on the route's
            # landing day, both. The `cstr` lend keeps panel 122's wider rule;
            # this clause is the field's own, and the wrapper §4.19 prescribes
            # takes the RECORD and lends inside itself, which stays legal.
            if !into[at].is_err() && !lend_extent.callee_is_extern(a, r, decls, call: into[at].must())
                state.push_diagnostic(@c, lend_errors.field_lend_needs_a_header(a.exprs[at].span))

            # **AND THE PARAMETER IT LANDS ON MUST DECLARE THE EXTENT** (panel
            # 166's route C, ratified 2026-09-19). The number a lend crosses
            # with is the CALL's word, and `f(p: ptr, n: i64)` relates the two
            # parameters not at all, so an overstated one read and WROTE past
            # the field at exit 0 — defect 063. `counted_by n` is the relation,
            # and absence is a refusal rather than silence: a check that fired
            # only where somebody wrote the mark would leave the corruption
            # standing wherever nobody did.
            #
            # It is asked AFTER the header question above, so a lend that
            # reaches no header meets the one message that explains why, and
            # `check/lend_extent.hero` answers absent for that case rather than
            # both firing.
            if !into[at].is_err()
                uncounted = lend_extent.uncounted_landing(a, r, s, decls, call: into[at].must(), arg: at)

                if !uncounted.is_err()
                    state.push_diagnostic(
                        @c,
                        lend_errors.field_lend_uncounted(callee: uncounted.must().callee, parameter: uncounted.must().parameter, span: a.exprs[at].span)
                    )
        at @ at + 1

# Whether a `ptr` lend's receiver is a place: `s.name`, `s.inner.name`, and
# never a call's result, a literal or an index. The root is a NAME, which is the
# one shape whose storage outlives the call the lend stands in.
function lends_a_place(a: ast.Ast, at: i64) -> bool
    match a.exprs[at].kind
        .method m => return rooted_at_a_name(a, m.receiver)
        .call cl => return cl.args.len() == 1 && rooted_at_a_name(a, cl.args[0].value)
        .name | .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit => return false
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index => return false
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => return false

function rooted_at_a_name(a: ast.Ast, at: i64) -> bool
    match a.exprs[at].kind
        .field fl => return rooted_at_a_name(a, fl.base)
        .name => return true
        .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit | .call => return false
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field_name | .index | .method => return false
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => return false

function holds_a_lend(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved) -> bool
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < a.exprs.len()
        if is_lend(a, r, at)
            return true
        at @ at + 1

    return false

# Every clause fires, and the legal shape stays quiet. `check/ffi_sweep.hero`'s
# own test is the model, and the lowering loop is not decoration: R3 and R4
# read `written_types`, which `lower_ty` is what fills.
function swept(text: str) -> i64
    p = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(p.tree, s, p.decls, p.uses)
    c: state.Checker @ state.new_checker(out_of: out, expr_count: p.tree.exprs.len())
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < p.tree.types.len()
        _ = lower.lower_ty(@c, p.tree, out, i)
        i @ i + 1

    lent_only_into_c(@c, p.tree, out, s, p.decls)
    return c.out.diagnostics.len()

test "a lend bound instead of passed is refused"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    c = x.cstr()\n    return n(s: c)\n") == 1

test "a Heroes function may not answer cstr"
    assert swept("function f(x: str) -> cstr\n    return x.cstr()\n") == 2

test "a record outside a group may not hold a cstr"
    assert swept("record H\n    c: cstr\n\nfunction f(h: H) -> H\n    return h\n") == 1

test "the legal shape stays quiet: a lend as an argument, and a group's own record"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record C\n        name: cstr\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    return n(s: x.cstr())\n") == 0

# Panel 164's two clauses, each made to fire and each shown quiet on the shape
# beside it. The legal case is third on purpose: a rule with no quiet case is a
# rule nobody can tell from "refuse everything".
test "a lent field kept in a binding is refused"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    k: ptr @ s.b.ptr()\n    return f(p: k, n: 4)\n") == 1

test "a lent field of a value nothing holds is refused"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n    function make() -> S\n\nfunction g() -> i64\n    return f(p: make().b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 1

test "a lent field passed straight into C stays quiet"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 0

# A field reached through another field is still a place, which is the shape
# beside the one that provoked the rule (CL-061).
test "a lent field nested one record deep is still a place"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record I\n        b: u8[4]\n    record S\n        i: I\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.i.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 0

# Panel 166's clause: the callee must be the group's. A wrapper taking `ptr`
# and a function value are the two ways a lend used to reach C with no header
# asked; the wrapper that takes the record is the legal shape beside them.
test "a lent field handed to a function of this program is refused, through a name and through a value"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction wrap(p: ptr, n: i64) -> i64\n    return f(p: p, n: n)\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return wrap(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 1
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction wrap(p: ptr, n: i64) -> i64\n    return f(p: p, n: n)\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    h = wrap\n    return h(s.b.ptr(), 4)\n") == 1

test "a wrapper that takes the record and lends inside itself stays quiet"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction total(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return total(s: s)\n") == 0
